    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Cleaners handle hazardous products at many client sites. The COSHH Regulations 2002 apply to every one. Here is what to assess and how to keep the records up to date.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Every hazardous cleaning product needs an assessment, however common it is.</li><li>Mixing products can make harmful gas. Bleach with an acid cleaner is the classic example.</li><li>Cleaners at client sites need the information before they use the product, not months later.</li><li>HSE says do not automatically choose PPE as the control.</li><li>Quickler files a COSHH Record per product from the sheet and what you say. It does not write the assessment for you.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The rule",
      "heading": "What the law asks",
      "html": "<p><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2002/2677/contents\">the COSHH Regulations 2002</a> apply to every employer whose people meet hazardous substances. <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/basics/assessment.htm\">HSE: how to carry out a COSHH risk assessment</a> sets the steps: identify, assess, control, inform and train, record, review. HSE keeps cleaning-related advice under <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/industry/index.htm\">HSE: COSHH and your industry</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The products",
      "heading": "What a cleaning firm holds",
      "html": "<p>Bleach and other disinfectants, descalers, caustic oven and drain cleaners, degreasers, floor strippers, solvent-based polishes. They irritate or burn skin and eyes, and some give off harmful vapour. Mixing them is a risk in its own right, so say on the assessment what must never be mixed.</p>"
    },
